1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:30,900
Let‘s be honest — I’m sure we‘ve all done something a little dishonest in our lives — maybe copying someone’s work, borrowing some money from a friend and not paying them back or being given too much change in a shop and keeping quiet about it.
老实说——我敢肯定我们在生活中都做过一些有点不诚实的事情——也许是抄袭别人的作业，向朋友借了钱却不还，或者在商店里收到多找的零钱却默不作声。

2
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:39,540
Or maybe I‘m wrong, maybe you’re a good, upstanding citizen who believes that honesty is the best policy.
又或许我错了，也许你是一个优秀、正直的公民，相信诚实才是最好的处事原则。

3
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:48,640
This proverb, ‘honesty is the best policy‘, was written back in the 1700s by American statesman, Benjamin Franklin.
“诚实才是上策”这句谚语是美国政治家本杰明·富兰克林在18世纪写的。

4
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:57,400
We may agree that owning up to our mistakes and behaving in a genuine and decent way, is the right way to lead our lives.
我们可能都认同，承认自己的错误，以真诚的和正直的方式行事，才是正确的生活方式。

5
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:02,670
But is there something in our human nature that makes us unscrupulous?
但我们的人性中是否存在一些东西让我们变得肆无忌惮呢？

6
00:01:02,670 --> 00:01:16,740
For example, in some shops and car parks you can find an honesty box — a box where you pay for something by putting money in it — but it relies on you putting the right amount of cash in.
例如，在一些商店和停车场，你会看到一个诚信箱——一个你通过往里面放钱来支付东西费用的箱子——但这依赖于你放入正确数额的现金。

7
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:27,070
This relies on people being honest and not trying to cheat but it does provide a temptation to get away with not paying the full amount.
这依赖于人们的诚实，不试图欺骗，但这确实提供了一种诱惑，让人有可能不付全款就蒙混过关。

8
00:01:27,070 --> 00:01:35,950
Philip Graves, a psychologist, and author of the book Consumerology, suggests that this temptation is part of our evolution.
心理学家、《消费学》一书的作者菲利普·格雷夫斯认为，这种诱惑是我们进化的一部分。

9
00:01:35,950 --> 00:01:40,800
He says: “We have evolved with the capacity to be dishonest.
他说：“我们在进化过程中具备了不诚实的能力。

10
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:51,260
It‘s part of our evolutionary psychological make up — because if we gain an advantage over the people around us, we have a greater chance of surviving.”
这是我们进化心理构成的一部分——因为如果我们比周围的人获得优势，我们就有更大的生存机会。”

11
00:01:51,260 --> 00:01:58,130
So why do we place such importance on being honest if we benefit from being dishonest?
那么，如果我们能从不诚实中获益，为什么我们还如此重视诚实呢？

12
00:01:58,130 --> 00:02:04,500
It’s because it is selfish behaviour — which other members of our social group won‘t like.
这是因为不诚实是一种自私的行为——我们社会群体中的其他成员不会喜欢这种行为。

13
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:12,570
If everybody acted selfishly and dishonestly all the time, the world would be a very unpleasant place.
如果每个人一直都自私地、不诚实地行事，这个世界将会变得非常令人不快。

14
00:02:12,570 --> 00:02:23,500
As Philip Graves says: “There is a balance to strike between the extent to which we can feather our own nest, so to speak, and the risk of being ostracised by the group.”
正如菲利普·格雷夫斯所说：“可以这么说，在我们中饱私囊的程度和被群体排斥的风险之间，需要找到一个平衡点。”

15
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:30,560
So, for societies to work together we need to trust each other and therefore we need to be honest.
所以，为了让社会能够协同运转，我们需要相互信任，因此我们需要诚实。

16
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:41,520
Being trustworthy with money is of course crucial for an economy to survive and there are laws to protect us from dishonest practices like corruption and fraud.
在金钱方面值得信赖对于经济的生存当然至关重要，而且有法律保护我们免受像贪污和欺诈这样不诚实的行为的侵害。

17
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:49,750
But being honest with words is another matter; saying what we think to someone can get us into hot water.
但在言语上诚实则是另一回事；对别人说出我们的想法可能会让我们陷入麻烦。

18
00:02:49,750 --> 00:02:59,960
However a new anonymous messaging app has been designed that lets anyone with a link to your profile to send you a message without knowing who it’s from.
然而，一款新的匿名信息应用程序被设计出来了，它让任何有你个人资料链接的人都可以给你发信息，而你却不知道信息来自谁。

19
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:12,510
The app now has 300 million users which perhaps indicates how honest we really like to be — but in all honesty, do you want to know what people think about you?
这款应用现在有3亿用户，这也许表明了我们到底有多希望自己诚实——但说实在的，你想知道人们是怎么看待你的吗？

20
00:03:12,510 --> 00:03:15,700
Is honesty always the best policy?!
诚实真的永远是上策吗？！
